## 1. Scope — what C3 does, per branch

C3 is the operational containment-verification gate. In branches (a), (b), and (d) it performs **operator transitions only** (state writes via the operator route `POST /v2/d6-containment` plus passive read-backs) and **one physical stop act against the then-current registration** — whichever registration the branch's census establishes. The stop act is **subtractive**: it observes the registration, deletes it, and reads back. In every branch C3 **sends no command, induces no wake, delivers no pass, and manufactures no traffic of any kind.** An operator containment transition is not an alarm wake: it does not deliver a pass, does not evaluate the schedule, and registers nothing — `contain_drive` and `clear` touch no alarm API at all, and `stop_chain`'s only alarm-API acts are the subtractive `deleteAlarm()` and `getAlarm()` reads. (In branches (a)/(d) the chain is already live and passes occur on their own schedule; C3 neither provokes nor needs them — §3, RB-7.)

